The Dirty Pipe vulnerability is a kernel-level buffer overflow vulnerability that affects the Linux kernel. It allows an attacker to overflow a buffer in the kernel and execute arbitrary code. Here's a simple example of how to exploit this vulnerability in a Linux environment.

### Step 1: Create a Vulnerable Kernel Module

First, we need to create a kernel module that contains the Dirty Pipe vulnerability. Here's a simple example:

```c
#include <linux/module.h>
#include <linux/kernel.h>
#include <linux/init.h>
#include <linux/sched.h>
#include <linux/slab.h>

#define BUFFER_SIZE 1024

static char *dirty_pipe_buffer;

static int __init dirty_pipe_init(void) {
    dirty_pipe_buffer = kmalloc(BUFFER_SIZE, GFP_KERNEL);
    if (!dirty_pipe_buffer) {
        pr_err("Failed to allocate buffer\n");
        return -ENOMEM;
    }

    pr_info("Dirty Pipe buffer allocated\n");

    return 0;
}

static void __exit dirty_pipe_exit(void) {
    kfree(dirty_pipe_buffer);
    pr_info("Dirty Pipe buffer freed\n");
}

module_init(dirty_pipe_init);
module_exit(dirty_pipe_exit);

MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
MODULE_AUTHOR("Your Name");
MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Dirty Pipe Vulnerability Example");
```

### Step 2: Compile the Kernel Module

Compile the kernel module using the following command:

```bash
make -C /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build M=$(pwd) modules
```

### Step 3: Load the Kernel Module

Load the kernel module into the kernel:

```bash
sudo insmod dirty_pipe.ko
```

